  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1108 AM MDT Sat Apr 18 2026\n\nCold front is now south of the CWA with some decent north winds \nbehind it. Continued pressure rises just east of our area should \nallow winds to turn more east throughout today. Strongest winds \neast/southeast starting early tonight into Sunday morning before \ndiminishing some later Sunday. Expect winds of 15-25 mph with gusts \nto 30 mph during this overnight period, and gusts as high as 40 mph \npossible along west slopes of mountains. \n\nThis surge in southeast winds will bring some low level moisture \ninto the area Sunday. Aloft, a weak disturbance just west of the \nBaja, will move up to the CWA later Sunday. These two features will \nallow for a slight chance of showers Sunday afternoon/night.\nMoisture axis will slowly lift northeast with the upper disturbance \nMonday. This will keep a chance of showers going, though mostly \nlimited to the mountains of the north. \n\nTuesday through Saturday...short wave ridge moves in Tuesday behind \nthe exiting upper disturbance. This will flush moisture out of the \nCWA and clear the skies. Next Pacific system moves to NoCal Tuesday, \nGreat Basin Wednesday, and then crosses the central Rockies \nThursday. This ushers in a stretch of dry, warm weather into the \nweekend, with winds gradually increasing each day. Breezy on \nWednesday with west winds of 15-25 mph, increasing Thursday to west \n20-30 mph with higher gusts. Winds diminishing some Friday and \nSaturday but still breezy in the afternoons. Fire conditions will \nslowly worsen through the week.",
